      faq: {
        id: 'faq',
        title: 'Frequently Asked Questions',
        faqs: [
          { q: 'What is TensorRT-LLM?', a: 'TensorRT-LLM is a free, open-source (Apache 2.0) library published by NVIDIA for optimizing and running large language model inference on NVIDIA GPUs. It was originally built on NVIDIA\'s TensorRT deep-learning inference SDK; release 1.1 and earlier offered a TensorRT backend that compiled models into GPU-specific engines, and in release 1.2 and later PyTorch is the sole execution backend.' },
          { q: 'Is TensorRT-LLM free?', a: 'Yes. TensorRT-LLM itself is free, open-source software released under the Apache 2.0 license. NVIDIA NIM, a separate prebuilt microservice layer that packages an open-source engine (vLLM or SGLang in the current NVIDIA documentation; earlier releases bundled several backends, per [NVIDIA\'s NIM for LLMs overview](https://docs.nvidia.com/nim/large-language-models/latest/about-nim-llm/overview.html)), is free for NVIDIA Developer Program members for research, development and experimentation (up to 16 GPUs per [NVIDIA\'s NIM FAQ](https://docs.api.nvidia.com/nim/docs/product)); production use requires an NVIDIA AI Enterprise license.' },
          { q: 'Does TensorRT-LLM run on AMD or Apple GPUs?', a: 'No. TensorRT-LLM runs exclusively on NVIDIA GPUs — there is no CPU-only, AMD, Intel, or Apple Silicon backend, unlike vLLM or llama.cpp, which support broader hardware.' },
          { q: 'Does TensorRT-LLM still require compiling a model first?', a: 'Not on current releases. Through release 1.1, the TensorRT backend built a model ahead of time into an engine with kernel selections fixed for a specific GPU architecture generation, trading a build step for an engine that NVIDIA describes as tuned for that GPU; PyTorch had been the default backend since release 1.0. NVIDIA\'s [release notes](https://nvidia.github.io/TensorRT-LLM/release-notes.html) list the TensorRT backend as removed in release 1.2, with PyTorch as the sole execution backend, so check the version you deploy.' },
          { q: 'What quantization formats does TensorRT-LLM support?', a: 'TensorRT-LLM supports FP8 and FP4 (FP4 exclusive to Blackwell-generation GPUs), INT8 and INT4 including SmoothQuant, and community formats like AWQ and GPTQ, with exact availability depending on the target GPU generation.' },
          { q: 'Is TensorRT-LLM better than vLLM?', a: '"Better" depends on the job: TensorRT-LLM aims for high per-GPU throughput on NVIDIA hardware specifically, at the cost of NVIDIA-only support (and, up to release 1.1, an ahead-of-time compile step). vLLM loads models directly with no compile step and documents backend support beyond NVIDIA GPUs alone. Neither is universally faster — see the comparison table above.' },
          { q: 'What is the difference between TensorRT-LLM and NVIDIA NIM?', a: 'TensorRT-LLM is the free, open-source library you operate yourself. NVIDIA NIM is a separate, prebuilt microservice layer that packages an open-source engine (vLLM or SGLang in the current NVIDIA documentation; earlier releases bundled several backends, per [NVIDIA\'s NIM for LLMs overview](https://docs.nvidia.com/nim/large-language-models/latest/about-nim-llm/overview.html)) with NVIDIA optimizations behind a standardized API with vendor support; production use requires an NVIDIA AI Enterprise license, while NVIDIA Developer Program members can use NIM free for development and experimentation (see [NVIDIA\'s NIM FAQ](https://docs.api.nvidia.com/nim/docs/product)).' },
          { q: 'Can TensorRT-LLM serve models across multiple GPUs?', a: 'Yes. NVIDIA documents multi-GPU and multi-node serving support in TensorRT-LLM for models too large to fit on a single GPU, typically deployed through NVIDIA Dynamo-Triton for production orchestration.' },
        ],
      },
